% Logical Thinking in the Age of AI, slide 7: "Within the Ethical Realm" -
% CONSTRUCTION AND DEFENSE OF ARGUMENTS: what is the reasoning behind this?
%
%   Leniency is in order towards a rapist who felt provoked into raping by the
%   victim's (devoid of actual aggression) attitude (e.g. seductive attire,
%   being in the "wrong" place, ...)
%
% To write the argument as rules we have to say what it rests on. Its
% conclusion needs a reason (the offender is less to blame), and that reason
% needs a premise the slogan never states: the victim shares the
% responsibility for the crime, because the victim's attitude provoked it -
% although the attitude involved no actual aggression.
%
% None of these rules mentions rape. The conditions are about an offender, a
% victim and the victim's attitude, so the argument transposes by itself:
% change three words in the facts (rapist -> murderer, raping -> killing,
% seductive attire -> defiant attire) and the same rules conclude
%
%   Leniency is in order towards a murderer who felt provoked into killing by
%   the victim's (devoid of actual aggression) attitude (e.g. defiant attire, ...)
%
% which nobody accepts. Scenarios rape_case and murder_case run query
% leniency on the two cases; the explanations are the same tree, word for
% word, but for those three words. Query hidden_premise shows the premise
% doing the work: which victim is held to share the responsibility.
% standard_for_judgment.le (slide 8) takes the argument one step further.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    leniency is in order towards *a person* for *a crime*.
    *a person* is less to blame for *a crime*.
    *a victim* shares the responsibility for *a crime*.
    *a person* committed *a crime* against *a victim*; undefined.
    *a person* felt provoked into *a crime* by *an attitude* of *a victim*; undefined.
    *an attitude* is devoid of actual aggression; undefined.

the knowledge base provocation_transposed includes:

% The argument, as stated ...
leniency is in order towards a person for a crime
    if the person is less to blame for the crime.

% ... rests on two premises that it does not state:
% (1) an offender who shares the responsibility for a crime with the victim
%     is less to blame for it;
a person is less to blame for a crime
    if the person committed the crime against a victim
    and the victim shares the responsibility for the crime.

% (2) a victim whose attitude provoked the offender shares the responsibility
%     for the crime, even when the attitude involved no actual aggression.
a victim shares the responsibility for a crime
    if a person committed the crime against the victim
    and the person felt provoked into the crime by an attitude of the victim
    and the attitude is devoid of actual aggression.

% The case of the argument.
scenario rape_case is:
    Ron committed rape against Vicky.
    Ron felt provoked into rape by seductive attire of Vicky.
    seductive attire is devoid of actual aggression.
    leniency expects answers ["leniency is in order towards Ron for rape"].
    hidden_premise expects answers ["Vicky shares the responsibility for rape"].

% The same case, transposed.
scenario murder_case is:
    Mark committed murder against Wendy.
    Mark felt provoked into murder by defiant attire of Wendy.
    defiant attire is devoid of actual aggression.
    leniency expects answers ["leniency is in order towards Mark for murder"].
    hidden_premise expects answers ["Wendy shares the responsibility for murder"].

% Both cases side by side: one argument, two conclusions.
scenario both_cases is:
    Ron committed rape against Vicky.
    Ron felt provoked into rape by seductive attire of Vicky.
    seductive attire is devoid of actual aggression.
    Mark committed murder against Wendy.
    Mark felt provoked into murder by defiant attire of Wendy.
    defiant attire is devoid of actual aggression.
    leniency expects answers [
        "leniency is in order towards Ron for rape",
        "leniency is in order towards Mark for murder"].
    hidden_premise expects answers [
        "Vicky shares the responsibility for rape",
        "Wendy shares the responsibility for murder"].

% Without the provocation the argument has nothing to stand on.
scenario no_provocation is:
    Ron committed rape against Vicky.
    leniency expects answers [].
    hidden_premise expects answers [].

query leniency is:
    leniency is in order towards which person for which crime.

query hidden_premise is:
    which victim shares the responsibility for which crime.
